 Tossup range.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Gus Bilirakis (R) is running."]}, "overview": "Gus Bilirakis, a Republican incumbent in a district that leans about seven points Republican, faces Democratic nominee Kimberly Overman and a minor independent candidate. Bilirakis has raised roughly $1.6 million and holds over $550,000 on hand, while Overman raised under $200,000 and had only about $5,000 remaining after winning her primary. Every major expert rating places the race at Safe or Likely Republican, and prediction markets give Bilirakis roughly an 88% chance of winning.\n\nThe race sits in the Likely Republican band rather than Safe Republican because the seat's lean is moderate by Florida standards, House Democrats have flagged the district as potentially in play, and only one partisan-sponsored poll exists, leaving limited direct evidence. Bilirakis's steady stream of legislative press releases and local law-enforcement endorsements reinforce his incumbency advantage heading into November.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0813, "margin": -8.73}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0907, "margin": -9.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.1053, "margin": -9.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0931, "margin": -9.0}]}